## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical implementation of privacy safeguards in Azure OpenAI within multi-tenant SaaS, focusing on data leakage prevention, RBAC, and auditable logging.

## Key Concepts
- PII detection and redaction pipelines
- Grounding with Cognitive Search over a versioned KB
- Per-tenant RBAC with Azure AD
- Data Lake auditing
- GDPR/CCPA compliance checks and synthetic-data tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: redaction before OpenAI call
async function process(input, tenantId) {
  const pii = detectPII(input);
  const redacted = redact(input, pii);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(redacted, tenantId);
  return await openai.complete(prompt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify no PII leaks in logs or prompts across tenants?
- What metrics ensure latency remains acceptable under load?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests secure, compliant Azure OpenAI usage for security incidents, focusing on input redaction, tenant isolation, and auditable data paths.

## Key Concepts
- Azure API Management, Functions, Key Vault
- Text Analytics redaction, data privacy
- Cognitive Search grounding against versioned KB in Blob
- Structured JSON playbooks and audit logging

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactInput(text, patterns){
  let out = text;
  for (const p of patterns){
    const re = new RegExp(p, 'g');
    out = out.replace(re, '[REDACTED]');
  }
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test redaction and grounding end-to-end?
- What metrics ensure latency, cost, and compliance targets are met?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests design for guardrails, data isolation, and cost controls in Azure OpenAI deployments, a common advanced requirement for regulated customers.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic safety policy engine
- Prompt redaction and blocking
- API Management routing
- Grounding with versioned KB (Blob + Cognitive Search)
- Per-tenant isolation (Cosmos DB) and quotas
- Telemetry and auditing (Data Lake)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal policy eval
function evaluatePrompt(prompt, policies) {
  for (const p of policies) {
    if (p.types?.some(t => prompt.includes(t))) {
      return {blocked: true, reason: p.reason}
    }
  }
  // simple redact example
  const redacted = prompt.replace(/SSN-\d{4}/g, '[REDACTED_SSN]')
  return {blocked: false, redacted}
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement per-tenant billing and quotas at API level?
- How would you test guardrails under burst traffic?

Why This Is Asked

